{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"e0e619d32c72814edf6213e3a13e66e0b9869f7d869ecbf7ea295594f7c524a9","slug":"filtering-facets-compression","location":"/chapters/ch04_index_for_text_retrieval/3_filtering_facets_compression.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Filtering, Facets, and Compression","authors":[{"id":"Roger Weber","name":"Roger Weber"}],"content_includes_title":false,"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"3_filtering_facets_compression.md","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/3_filtering_facets_c-7149c0f6fbeb814aafaf50c9b3f0c7b4.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Real queries rarely ask for text alone. A user searching a library wants “database systems” among books published after 2000, or filtered to the Computer Science shelf. And a production index must stay small enough to keep its hot data in memory. This section adds two capabilities to the inverted index: metadata filtering, including the faceted navigation built on top of it, and the compression that keeps postings compact.","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"E6nX1v2Cys"}],"key":"oiWYP6wqaY"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Filtering on metadata","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"ZHwUd6MR2d"}],"identifier":"filtering-on-metadata","label":"Filtering on metadata","html_id":"filtering-on-metadata","implicit":true,"key":"qj7h37QyiA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A predicate is a condition on document metadata, such as ","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"eVY5btsrXL"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"year > 2000","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"aJvanhdGhO"},{"type":"text","value":" or ","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"MkzDheY2hA"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"genre = \"Computer Science\"","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"Vj9swXvA8G"},{"type":"text","value":". There are three ways to combine such a predicate with a text query, differing in when and how the predicate is evaluated.","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"JJNxqafvM9"}],"key":"ogZBl10VjN"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A priori filtering","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"kvjkEDUOD5"}],"key":"T0w3diqscm"},{"type":"text","value":" evaluates the predicate first. We keep document metadata in a structure that answers the condition efficiently, a B-tree or an inverted list on the attribute, and look up the set of document identifiers that satisfy it. That set is passed into the retriever, which drops any candidate not in it during the postings merge. This is the best option when the predicate can be indexed, because it removes non-matching documents before scoring.","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"k0CRHPDolZ"}],"key":"chSnpWWkKG"}],"key":"VpHYFdKrra"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A posteriori filtering","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"ENh2dvVhBA"}],"key":"X0LyDSaGp1"},{"type":"text","value":" evaluates the predicate last. When no index exists for the attribute, we rank first and check the predicate only on the documents we are about to return, pulling from the top-","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"xhAXm0UJTj"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"k","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03148em;\">k</span></span></span></span>","key":"xek5wrwD1B"},{"type":"text","value":" heap in score order and skipping those that fail. For a loose predicate we test only the few documents we return plus a handful skipped. For a highly selective predicate we may have to walk deep into the heap, but that is still cheaper than testing every document in the collection.","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"YdtZYPE6pX"}],"key":"eahWnOiIvM"}],"key":"DhWGN0osli"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inline filtering","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"sRIlAGVPkv"}],"key":"rKlwBk3Pfo"},{"type":"text","value":" evaluates the predicate during the merge. We store the attribute in a stream aligned with the postings and ordered by document identifier, and advance it in lockstep with the postings streams, testing the condition as each candidate appears. This keeps filtering and scoring in a single pass.","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"FatGPK8XrE"}],"key":"xDTcbPLnsX"}],"key":"MFtTNLu12h"}],"key":"jsxnrGbE7d"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In each case the retrieval algorithm itself is unchanged. Filtering slots into the same place where relevance feedback already removes documents: a candidate that fails the predicate is simply not scored or not returned.","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"B9KKLdgxX7"}],"key":"AxqrGkmzJ5"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Faceted search","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"UjdkQnBED6"}],"identifier":"faceted-search","label":"Faceted search","html_id":"faceted-search","implicit":true,"key":"IPLOYqWVAl"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Faceted search is filtering turned into navigation. Instead of a free-form predicate, the user picks from categorical attributes, a genre, a publication decade, a content type, and the system both filters the results and reports how many documents fall under each option. This is the a priori approach specialized to categories: the index keeps a separate postings list for each facet value, so filtering by a facet is an intersection with that list, and the facet counts are the list lengths.","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"tkI5DuBa96"}],"key":"AjvbolsgU5"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two refinements reduce the storage this adds. ","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"gHTjeTj8FI"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clustered indexing","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"xWoE45zn5o"}],"key":"faA7Juu2ag"},{"type":"text","value":" groups documents that share facet values, so their postings lists overlap heavily and can be encoded relative to a shared reference list. ","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"Zl4ipbPaoS"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hierarchical facet compression","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"WAiCZBIgTP"}],"key":"NV4iPM42bd"},{"type":"text","value":" exploits nested facets: a geographic hierarchy like Country then State then City stores only the differences between levels rather than the full path for every document.","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"nCKmAtI41L"}],"key":"DI9DW8IYs2"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compressing postings lists","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"JjkqM2Z4kS"}],"identifier":"compressing-postings-lists","label":"Compressing postings lists","html_id":"compressing-postings-lists","implicit":true,"key":"XAO2kGlqwH"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compression is one of the highest-leverage optimizations in an inverted index. Cutting postings storage by a factor of four or more lets more of the index sit in memory, and reading fewer bytes from disk directly lowers query latency.","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"Hoy8wTRWNN"}],"key":"zrXUT4DG7Z"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The key observation is that a postings list is a sorted sequence of document identifiers. Rather than storing each identifier, we store the gaps between consecutive identifiers, the ","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"fSQ7ZOtZPN"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"d-gaps","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"keJO47u2A3"}],"key":"wKtmNTht2V"},{"type":"text","value":". In a list like 95673, 127088, ... we store the first identifier and then the gap 31415. Gaps are much smaller than absolute identifiers, especially for frequent terms whose postings are dense, and small integers compress well.","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"wdyjBmIkfF"}],"key":"L3NWO81i0f"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Variable-byte encoding","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"IPhqR99RqB"}],"identifier":"variable-byte-encoding","label":"Variable-byte encoding","html_id":"variable-byte-encoding","implicit":true,"key":"x8sQOVlKlC"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Variable-byte (VByte) encoding is the most widely used byte-aligned scheme. It uses the most significant bit of each byte as a continuation flag and the remaining seven bits for data. Ev